    "source": "Workshop 3 slides · Financial and non-financial reporting",
    "provider": "ICAS",
    "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source workshop-facsimile\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-workshop-additions/ws-source-063.png\" width=\"1728\" height=\"972\" alt=\"Question 3\nGive two examples of differences between financial and non-financial \nreporting disclosures.\n \nICAS ARP Workshop 3\n17\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source workshop-facsimile\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-workshop-additions/ws-source-064.png\" width=\"1728\" height=\"972\" alt=\"Question 3 – answer \nFinancial vs impact materiality\n• Financial reporting has a distinct financial materiality perspective \n• Non-financial reporting has a complementary and broader role and applies the \nimpact materiality perspective. \nDefinition of reporting entity\n• Financial reporting follows certain rules related to the recognition of a \nconsolidated entity or investment under the umbrella of a reporting entity. \n• Non-financial reporting follows a broader approach. It has the reporting entity as \na starting point but, depending on the type of disclosure or the framework \nfollowed, adds additional interested parties.\nICAS ARP Workshop 3\n18\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source workshop-facsimile\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-workshop-additions/ws-source-065.png\" width=\"1728\" height=\"972\" alt=\"Question 3 – answer (cont&#x27;d)\nTime horizon​\n• Financial reporting presents the entity’s current financial position and past \nfinancial performance.\n• Non-financial reporting adopts a more forward-looking approach. For instance, \nsustainability considerations usually need significant time to be realised and \nimplemented.\nMonetary considerations\n• Financial reporting is clearly expressed in monetary terms.\n• Non-financial reporting usually expresses non-monetary considerations \nassociated with the broader environment and the entity’s business model.\nICAS ARP Workshop 3\n19\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source workshop-facsimile\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-workshop-additions/ws-source-066.png\" width=\"1728\" height=\"972\" alt=\"Question 3 – answer (cont.) \nReliability\n• Financial reporting includes information that has a high degree of reliability in \nterms of measurement and recognition while also providing detailed disclosures \non sources of uncertainty.\n• Non-financial reporting has a high degree of uncertainty, mainly due to its \nforward-looking nature and limited guidance over measurement and recognition.\nICAS ARP Workshop 3\n20\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
